          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "NADH and NADPH Are Important Electron Carriers 73  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "There Are Many Other Activated Carrier Molecules in Cells 75  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Synthesis of Biological Polymers Is Driven by ATP Hydrolysis 76  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 78  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "HOW CELLS OBTAIN ENERGY FROM FOOD 80  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Glycolysis Is a Central ATP-producing Pathway 80  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Glycolysis Illustrates How Enzymes Couple Oxidation to Energy Storage 83  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fermentations Produce ATP in the Absence of Oxygen 84  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Organisms Store Food Molecules in Special Reservoirs 85  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Between Meals, Most Animal Cells Derive Their Energy from Fatty Acids Obtained from Fat 86  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Sugars and Fats Are Both Degraded to Acetyl CoA in Mitochondria 87  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Citric Acid Cycle Generates NADH by Oxidizing Acetyl Groups to "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\mathrm{CO}_{2}"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": " 88  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Electron Transport Drives the Synthesis of the Majority of the ATP in Most Cells 90  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Many Biosynthetic Pathways Begin with Glycolysis or the Citric Acid Cycle 90  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Animals Must Obtain All the Nitrogen and Sulfur They Need from Food 91  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Metabolism Is Highly Organized and Regulated 92  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 93  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Problems 112  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "References 114"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.078,
            0.418,
            0.218,
            0.433
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter 3 Proteins"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "index",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.076,
            0.437,
            0.474,
            0.931
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "THE ATOMIC STRUCTURE OF PROTEINS 115  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Structure of a Protein Is Specified by Its Amino Acid Sequence 115  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Proteins Fold into a Conformation of Lowest Energy 121  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\alpha"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": " Helix and the "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\beta"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": " Sheet Are Common Folding Motifs 121  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Four Levels of Organization Are Considered to Contribute to Protein Structure 123  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Protein Domains Are the Modular Units from Which Larger Proteins Are Built 124  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Proteins Also Contain Unstructured Regions 126  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "All Protein Structures Are Dynamic, Interconverting Rapidly Between an Ensemble of Closely Related Conformations Because of Thermal Energy 126  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Function Has Selected for a Tiny Fraction of the Many Possible Polypeptide Chains 126  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Proteins Can Be Classified into Many Families 127  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Some Protein Domains Are Found in Many Different Proteins 129  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Human Genome Encodes a Complex Set of Proteins, Revealing That Much Remains Unknown 130  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Protein Molecules Often Contain More Than One Polypeptide Chain 130  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Some Globular Proteins Form Long Helical Filaments 131  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Protein Molecules Can Have Elongated, Fibrous Shapes 132  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Covalent Cross-Linkages Stabilize Extracellular Proteins 133  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Protein Molecules Often Serve as Subunits for the Assembly of Large Structures 134  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Many Structures in Cells Are Capable of Self-Assembly 136  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Assembly Factors Often Aid the Formation of Complex Biological Structures 136  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "When Assembly Processes Go Wrong: The Case of Amyloid Fibrils 137  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Amyloid Structures Can Also Perform Useful Functions in Cells 139  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 140  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "PROTEIN FUNCTION 140  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "All Proteins Bind to Other Molecules 140  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Surface Conformation of a Protein Determines Its Chemistry 142  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Sequence Comparisons Between Protein Family Members Highlight Crucial Ligand-binding Sites 142  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Proteins Bind to Other Proteins Through Several Types of Interfaces 143  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Antibody Binding Sites Are Especially Versatile 144  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Equilibrium Constant Measures Binding Strength 145  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Enzymes Are Powerful and Highly Specific Catalysts 146  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Substrate Binding Is the First Step in Enzyme Catalysis 146"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "index",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.492,
            0.099,
            0.891,
            0.635
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Enzymes Speed Reactions by Selectively Stabilizing Transition States 148  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Enzymes Can Use Simultaneous Acid and Base Catalysis 148  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Lysozyme Illustrates How an Enzyme Works 149  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Tightly Bound Small Molecules Add Extra Functions to Proteins 152  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Cell Regulates the Catalytic Activities of Its Enzymes 155  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Allosteric Enzymes Have Two or More Binding Sites That Interact 155  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Two Ligands Whose Binding Sites Are Coupled Must Reciprocally Affect Each Other's Binding 157  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Symmetrical Protein Assemblies Produce Cooperative Allosteric Transitions 158  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Many Changes in Proteins Are Driven by Protein Phosphorylation 159  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Eukaryotic Cell Contains a Large Collection of Protein Kinases and Protein Phosphatases 159  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Regulation of the Src Protein Kinase Reveals How a Protein Can Function as a Microprocessor 161  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Regulatory GTP-binding Proteins Are Switched On and Off by the Gain and Loss of a Phosphate Group 162  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Proteins Can Be Regulated by the Covalent Addition of Other Proteins 162  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "An Elaborate Ubiquitin-conjugating System Is Used to Mark Proteins 163  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Protein Complexes with Interchangeable Parts Make Efficient Use of Genetic Information 164  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A GTP-binding Protein Shows How Large Protein Movements Can Be Generated from Small Ones 166  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Motor Proteins Produce Directional Movement in Cells 167  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Proteins Often Form Large Complexes That Function as Protein Machines 167  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Disordered Regions in Proteins Are Critical for a Set of Different Functions 168  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Scaffolds Bring Sets of Interacting Macromolecules Together and Concentrate Them in Selected Regions of a Cell 170  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Macromolecules Can Self-assemble to Form Biomolecular Condensates 171  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Classical Studies of Phase Separation Have Relevance for Biomolecular Condensates 173  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Comparison of Three Important Types of Large Biological Assemblies 174  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Many Proteins Are Controlled by Covalent Modifications That Direct Them to Specific Sites Inside the Cell 175  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Complex Network of Protein Interactions Underlies Cell Function 176  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Protein Structures Can Be Predicted and New Proteins Designed 178  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 179  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Problems 179  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "References 181"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "index",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.493,
            0.65,
            0.89,
            0.933
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Chapter 4 DNA, Chromosomes, and Genomes 183  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "THE STRUCTURE AND FUNCTION OF DNA 185  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A DNA Molecule Consists of Two Complementary Chains of Nucleotides 185  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Structure of DNA Provides a Mechanism for Heredity 187  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "In Eukaryotes, DNA Is Enclosed in a Cell Nucleus 189  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 189  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "CHROMOSOMAL DNA AND ITS PACKAGING IN THE CHROMATIN FIBER 189  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Eukaryotic DNA Is Packaged into a Set of Chromosomes 190  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Chromosomes Contain Long Strings of Genes 191  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Nucleotide Sequence of the Human Genome Shows How Our Genes Are Arranged 193  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Each DNA Molecule That Forms a Linear Chromosome Must Contain a Centromere, Two Telomeres, and Replication Origins 195  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "DNA Molecules Are Highly Condensed in Chromosomes 197  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Nucleosomes Are a Basic Unit of Eukaryotic Chromosome Structure 197  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Structure of the Nucleosome Core Particle Reveals How DNA Is Packaged 198  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Nucleosomes Have a Dynamic Structure and Are Frequently Subjected to Changes Catalyzed by ATP-dependent Chromatin-remodeling Complexes 200"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
